# Documents and modules

## The prologue {#prologue}

A document starts with its *prologue*: the commands before its first content. Imports, variables, and the Lua that defines syntax go there. The prologue ends at the first other block.

```
:import std.toc
:set author = Ada
:export nav.category = Guides

# The title
```

Comments do not end the prologue, so a file can start with a comment that says what it is.

## Including {#include}

`:include` puts another file's content where it is, as part of this document:

```
:include parts/intro
```

The path is relative to the document, or to the workspace's root with a leading `/`; `.nml` may be left out. The included file sees the document's grammar and variables, and what it names belongs to the document: its headings are numbered with the document's, and referenced as the document's.

Includes are for parts of one document, and for what many pages share: this documentation's pages each include a file with their sidebar.

## Pages and libraries {#libraries}

Every `.nml` file of the workspace is a *page*, written to the output, unless the [[configuration#pages|configuration]] says otherwise. The other files are *libraries*: they are checked, but have no page. Files that are only included, and modules, are libraries:

```
pages = ["**/*.nml"]
exclude = ["parts/**", "lib/**"]
```

## Modules {#import}

A module is a library whose syntax, Lua and exported variables a document takes with `:import`, in its prologue:

```
:import std.toc
:import lib.glossary as g
```

The module's Lua runs in its own namespace, `g` here (by default, the module's last name), and its exported variables are `%g.name%`. The rules it defines — its syntax — become part of the document's grammar. Unlike an include, an import puts no content in the document.

Modules are found in the roots the configuration names: `:import lib.glossary` is `<root of lib>/glossary.nml`.

```
[[module]]
name = "lib"
root = "lib"
```

`std` is NML's standard library, built into `nml`: [[std#]] describes its modules.

A module documents itself with the comments it starts with, in Markdown: editors show them when the import is hovered or completed.

## Building again {#dependencies}

A page is built again when a file it includes or imports changes, and relinked when what its references point to changes. See [[building#]].
